  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DISPERSAL: A KEY TO REFILING SOUTHEAST ASIA S LARGEST TIGER LANDSCAPE</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO (1) UNDERSTAND TIGER DISPERSAL POTENTIAL IN THE WESTERN FOREST COMPLEX (WEFCOM) USING A LANDSCAPE MODEL DEVELOPED TO INTEGRATE DISPERSAL MOVEMENTS, PREDICT TIGER AND PREY OCCUPANCY AND IDENTIFY AREAS FOR MANAGEMENT (2) CREATE A TIGER DATA DATABASE AT KHAO NANG RAM RESEARCH STATION FOR THE PERIOD 2007 TO PRESENT TO ESTABLISH A COMMON IDENTIFICATION SYSTEM LINKING COLLARED TIGER IDS FROM MULTIPLE PROJECTS AND INCORPORATE INTO AN EXISTING WEFCOM SPATIAL DATABASE COMPOSED OF THE PROBABILITY OF HABITAT OCCUPANCY BY TIGERS AND THEIR PRIMARY PREY (3) DEVELOP AND CONDUCT CONSERVATION EDUCATION WORKSHOPS AT NINE KEY KAREN VILLAGES IN THUNG YAI EAST AND WEST TO BUILD A PROGRAM TO INCREASE COMMUNITY CONSERVATION AND REDUCE SUBSISTENCE HUNTING AND (4) DEVELOP AND CONDUCT TWO WORKSHOPS WITH PROTECTED AREA CHIEFS, REGIONAL DNP RESEARCH STAFF, LOCAL AND INTERNATIONAL NGOS TO CREATE A STRATEGIC PLAN TO EXPAND THE DISTRIBUTION OF TIGER BREEDING HABITAT TO THE 63% OF WEFCOM WHERE TIGERS ARE NOT CURRENTLY BREEDING.</narrative>
  </description>
